  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 AM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost Advisory in effect through mid-morning today \nfor south-central VA and interior portions of southeast VA and \nnortheast NC. Dry and seasonably mild otherwise through mid-week. \n\nChilly early this morning with temperatures in the upper 30s to \nlower 40s. Calm winds and mostly clear skies are allowing for decent \nradiational cooling conditions, though the position of the nearby \nhigh pressure system isn't 100% ideal for a very cold night. \nRegardless, am expecting temps to drop a few more degrees through \nsunrise. Frost is possible away from the coast, though will tend to \nfocused in rural and sheltered locations. A Frost Advisory is in \neffect for interior southern VA and NE NC where the growing season \nis still ongoing. \n\nAfter the chilly morning, temps should warm quickly, leading to a \nvery nice day with clear skies, a SW wind, and highs in upper 60s to \nlower 70s. Not as cold tonight with lows in the 40s for most. \nAnother seasonably mild day is on the way for Wednesday with \nsky cover and temperatures very similar to today. Could see some\nincrease in clouds late in the day as low pressure begins to \ntake shape well NW of the region. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front brings chances for rain Thursday-\nFriday, but any potential for appreciable rain is low. Much \ncooler temperatures follow behind the front. \n\nA big change remains on the horizon as we approach late week. A \nshortwave trough is forecast to dive southward out of the north-\ncentral CONUS Wednesday, with a larger longwave trough and embedded \nupper low setting up shop over most of the eastern CONUS by Thursday \nand Friday. While some precip is possible Thursday and Friday, the \nmain focus will be the much cooler temperatures that overspread the \narea behind the associated frontal passage. Thursday looks to be the \nlast \"mild\" day for a while with pre-frontal high temps in the mid \n60s NW to lower 70s SE. A series of cold fronts then crosses \nthe local area Thursday evening and overnight, bringing those \nmuch cooler temps by Friday. At this time, forecast highs Friday\nare only in the 50s, with lower 50s for the northern and \nnorthwest tier of our CWA (mainly N and NW of a Farmville-\nRichmond- Salisbury line). The large upper trough remains parked\nover most of the eastern CONUS Saturday and this still looks \nlike the coolest day of the period: highs only in the lower 50s \nfor the entire area and even some potential for upper 40s across\nthe N and especially the lower MD Eastern Shore. Some \nsemblance of troughing remains in place Sunday, so those cool \ntemps are expected to persist. Overnight lows also dip to around\nfreezing for most locations away from the coast, with even some\nupper 20s possible in the Piedmont. Gusty winds could make \nthese temps feel even chillier. A very gradual moderating trend \nis possible starting early next week. \n\nRegarding the precip potential Thursday-Friday, the synoptic pattern \nis not very favorable for appreciable rainfall. The sfc low \npasses W to E through the northern Mid-Atlantic Thursday, with \nthe main trough axis lagging well behind these sfc features. The\noverall moisture also appears limited with the westerly flow \naloft. Still, at least some light rain or showers are expected \nlater Thursday into Friday, particularly N of the I-64 corridor \nand especially on the lower MD Eastern Shore. Expected/forecast \nQPF is only on the order of a tenth or two per the latest NBM \nand ensemble guidance, and even these amounts could be on the \ngenerous side. Probs for a half inch of QPF have cratered to \n<5-10%, so any sort of beneficial rain is unlikely at this \npoint. Conditions dry out for the weekend, though those \naforementioned gusty (NW) winds are likely to persist in the \nFriday-Sunday timeframe as the pressure gradient remains \ncompressed over the area.",
